/* CIL 524: practice questions written from the 2025/26 materials:
   Classes 1–9 lecture notes + slide decks 1.1 Introduction, 1.2 Formation of Contract,
   1.3 Terms of Contract. Styled after the 2021/22 paper. */
BANKS["cil-new"] = {
  id: "cil-new", course: "CIL", kind: "new",
  title: "CIL 524 Practice",
  subtitle: "Written from the 2025/26 classes · Dr Ilobinso & Ms Adewumi",
  meta: "72 questions · Classes 1–9 and the slide decks",
  q: [
/* ── Sources & classification ── */
{t:"Sources of law",s:"Class 1",q:"The received English law is divided into three parts. They include all except",o:["Common law","Doctrines of equity","Statutes of general application","Customary law","None of the above"],a:3,
 w:"Customary law is a separate Nigerian source, sitting alongside case law and Nigerian legislation. The received English law itself is only common law, equity and statutes of general application."},
{t:"Sources of law",s:"Class 1",q:"A statute of general application is a law passed by the British Parliament",o:["before 1st January 1900","before 1st January 1960","after 1st January 1900","at any time, provided it is written","during the colonial period"],a:0,
 w:"The Sale of Goods Act 1893 is the working example; it still applies in Nigerian states that have not enacted their own Sale of Goods law."},
{t:"Sources of law",s:"Class 1",q:"The Sale of Goods Act 1893 does NOT apply in a Nigerian state where",o:["the state has fewer than five High Courts","the state has its own Sale of Goods Law on the same subject matter","the parties are not Nigerian citizens","the contract is oral","the goods are imported"],a:1,
 w:"Received English law yields to an existing Nigerian law on the same subject. Lagos, Edo/Delta and the old Western Region states have their own Sale of Goods laws."},
{t:"Sources of law",s:"Class 1",q:"The doctrines of equity were developed principally to",o:["replace the common law entirely","alleviate the hardship caused by the strict application of common law","codify judicial decisions into statutes","give the colonial courts jurisdiction","create a new system of criminal punishment"],a:1,
 w:"Common law is black and white; equity asks whether the result is just. ‘He that comes to equity must come with clean hands’ is one of its maxims."},
{t:"Sources of law",s:"Class 1",q:"The rule that a decision of a superior court must be followed by lower courts where the facts are the same or very similar is called",o:["Res judicata","Judicial precedence","Ultra vires","Ratio decidendi","Stare indicis"],a:1,
 w:"Judicial precedence (the rule of precedent) is what turns repeated judicial decisions into common law. Contract law is heavily precedent-based."},
{t:"Sources of law",s:"Class 1",q:"Which of these is the highest court in Nigeria?",o:["Court of Appeal","Federal High Court","Supreme Court","National Industrial Court","State High Court"],a:2,
 w:"Supreme Court, then Court of Appeal, then Federal High Courts, State High Courts, Magistrate Courts, and Customary and Sharia Courts."},
{t:"Sources of law",s:"Class 1",q:"The National Industrial Court is best described as a",o:["customary court","court of special jurisdiction","court of appeal","court of first instance for all civil matters","tribunal without judicial powers"],a:1,
 w:"It is a specialised court dealing with employment matters, like the Coroner’s Court for inquiries into death and the Juvenile Court."},
{t:"Foundations",s:"Class 1",q:"Contract law is best classified as",o:["public and criminal law","private and civil law","public and civil law","private and criminal law","administrative law"],a:1,
 w:"Contract law regulates dealings between private citizens and is civil, not criminal. Breaching a contract is not an arrestable offence: you go to court, not the police, unless fraud can be proved."},
{t:"Foundations",s:"Class 1",q:"Which statement is correct?",o:["All agreements are contracts","All contracts are agreements","Every contract must be in writing","Every agreement is legally binding","A gratuitous promise is always a contract"],a:1,
 w:"All contracts are agreements, but not all agreements are contracts: gratuitous promises, illegal contracts and defective consent all produce agreements that are not enforceable."},
{t:"Foundations",s:"Class 1",q:"Freedom of contract means that",o:["the court will set aside a bad bargain","parties are free to negotiate and agree their own terms without state interference","only written contracts are enforceable","the state fixes the price of every transaction","a party may withdraw from a contract at any time"],a:1,
 w:"If you sell your laptop for ₦450 and the other party agrees, the court will not ask whether that was wise. Its business is to enforce the bargain you actually made."},
{t:"Foundations",s:"Class 1",q:"The law has intervened in freedom of contract in modern times principally because of",o:["the volume of litigation","imbalance of bargaining power between mass producers and consumers","the abolition of customary law","the growth of the internet alone","the shortage of judges"],a:1,
 w:"Where goods are sold on a take-it-or-leave-it basis there is no real bargain, so the law imposes conditions to protect consumers, infants, illiterates, intoxicated persons and persons of unsound mind."},
{t:"Foundations",s:"Class 1",q:"The main practical significance of the distinction between a simple contract and a contract under seal is that",o:["a deed must be registered with the court","a deed does not require consideration","a simple contract cannot be enforced","a deed can only be made by a company","a simple contract must be witnessed"],a:1,
 w:"If someone signs and delivers a deed promising you their car, you can enforce it whether or not you gave anything in exchange."},
{t:"Foundations",s:"Class 1",q:"You walk into a restaurant, are seated, eat, and then try to leave without paying. The court will most likely hold that there was",o:["no contract, since nothing was said","an implied contract inferred from your conduct","a contract under seal","a unilateral contract","an invitation to treat only"],a:1,
 w:"Contracts can be formed by conduct as well as by words. Entering premises where food is sold for money and eating there is conduct from which agreement is inferred."},
{t:"Foundations",s:"Class 1 · Slide 1.2",q:"Carlill v. Carbolic Smoke Ball Co. is authority for the proposition that",o:["advertisements are always invitations to treat","an offer can be made to the whole world","consideration must be adequate","silence can amount to acceptance","a company cannot be sued in contract"],a:1,
 w:"The company promised money to anyone who used the smoke ball as directed and still caught influenza, and had deposited the money in a bank. Only one party made a promise, the classic unilateral contract."},
/* ── Offer & invitation to treat ── */
{t:"Formation",s:"Class 1 · Slide 1.2",q:"For a proposal to be considered an offer it must be",o:["clear, complete and definite","written and signed","made to a specific person only","supported by a deposit","approved by a lawyer"],a:0,
 w:"Once the court detects a lack of intention to be bound without further negotiation, the proposal is an invitation to treat rather than an offer."},
{t:"Formation",s:"Slide 1.2",q:"An advertisement stating that an auction will hold is",o:["an offer","an invitation to treat","an acceptance","a counter-offer","a consideration"],a:1,
 w:"Both the advertisement and the auctioneer’s call for bids are invitations to treat. Each bid is the offer, accepted only when the hammer falls; until then the bidder may withdraw."},
{t:"Formation",s:"Slide 1.2",q:"A request for tender is generally an invitation to treat. It will however constitute an offer where",o:["the tender is submitted in writing","the invitation explicitly states that the lowest or highest bid will be accepted","more than three tenderers respond","the project value exceeds a stated sum","a government agency is the party inviting"],a:1,
 w:"An express promise to accept the lowest or highest bid removes the need for further negotiation, so the invitation itself becomes capable of acceptance."},
{t:"Formation",s:"Slide 1.2",q:"In Harvey v. Facey (1893) the defendant’s telegram stating a price of £900 was held to be",o:["a valid offer","a mere supply of information","an acceptance","a counter-offer","a binding option"],a:1,
 w:"Quoting a price in answer to an enquiry supplies information; it does not indicate willingness to be bound without further negotiation."},
{t:"Formation",s:"Slide 1.2",q:"An advertisement inviting the public to purchase shares in a company is",o:["an offer, since it is called an offer of shares","an invitation to treat","an acceptance by the company","a unilateral contract","void for uncertainty"],a:1,
 w:"Despite being commonly called an ‘offer of shares’, the member of the public makes the offer by applying, and the company may or may not accept it."},
{t:"Formation",s:"Slide 1.2",q:"A transport timetable or itinerary constitutes",o:["an offer accepted by boarding","an invitation to treat","a contract under seal","a conditional acceptance","an implied term"],a:1,
 w:"The timetable does not commit the carrier to be bound without further steps; the contract is formed at a later point in the transaction."},
{t:"Formation",s:"Class 1",q:"A company mistakenly lists a television at £10 instead of £100 and thousands of orders are placed. The court held that a display of goods on a website is",o:["an offer, so every order is a binding contract","an invitation to treat, so the customer makes the offer","void for common mistake","voidable for undue influence","a unilateral contract to the whole world"],a:1,
 w:"The customer who placed the item in the cart made the offer; the company accepted only on taking payment and sending confirmation. Customers who had received confirmation were entitled to the goods, the rest were not."},
{t:"Formation",s:"Class 1",q:"At which point does an ATM transaction become an offer?",o:["When you stand in front of the machine considering it","When you insert your card","When the machine displays the menu of options","When you choose the action and confirm a specific amount","When you collect the receipt"],a:3,
 w:"Standing there, inserting the card and viewing options are all indefinite: you might only be checking your balance. The offer is made at the point of no return, when you confirm."},
/* ── Acceptance ── */
{t:"Acceptance",s:"Class 2",q:"Acceptance is best defined as",o:["any response to an offer","the final and unqualified assent to the terms of an offer","a promise to consider the offer","an acknowledgement of receipt of the offer","a request for further information"],a:1,
 w:"Any modification or addition to the terms amounts to a counter-offer, an invalid acceptance but a valid new offer."},
{t:"Acceptance",s:"Class 2",q:"Which of the following amounts to valid acceptance?",o:["Silence for thirty days","A statement acknowledging receipt of the offer","A request for further information","Unqualified assent communicated to the offeror","A counter-offer on better terms"],a:3,
 w:"Silence or inactivity is never acceptance, and a mere acknowledgement or an enquiry is not assent. Note that requesting further information does not destroy the offer either; it simply is not acceptance."},
{t:"Acceptance",s:"Class 2",q:"A counter-offer has which effect on the original offer?",o:["It suspends it for a reasonable time","It kills it, so the offeree cannot later accept it","It converts it into a deed","It has no effect at all","It makes it irrevocable"],a:1,
 w:"The counter-offer terminates the original offer. The offeree cannot change his mind and go back to the terms he rejected."},
{t:"Acceptance",s:"Class 2 · Slide 1.2",q:"Under the postal rule, acceptance sent by post takes effect",o:["when the offeror reads it","when it is received by the offeror","when it is posted","seven days after posting","only if the offeror acknowledges it"],a:2,
 w:"The rationale is that the law will not punish the offeree for delays beyond his control. In Adams v. Lindsell the contract came into existence on posting even though the goods were sold to a third party before the letter arrived."},
{t:"Acceptance",s:"Class 2 · Slide 1.2",q:"The postal rule does NOT apply to",o:["acceptance of an offer for the sale of wool","communication of revocation","acceptance where post is the usual mode","offers made to the whole world","acceptance by telegram"],a:1,
 w:"Revocation must actually reach the offeree to be effective. Otherwise an offeree could be bound by a withdrawal he never received."},
{t:"Acceptance",s:"Class 2 · Slide 1.2",q:"Where the offeror prescribes a particular mode of acceptance, for example by signing and returning an attached agreement,",o:["any mode of acceptance will do","the prescribed mode must be followed or the acceptance is invalid","the offer automatically lapses after 24 hours","acceptance may only be oral","the contract becomes a deed"],a:1,
 w:"Afolabi v. Polymera Industries Ltd is the illustration. Where no mode is prescribed, acceptance may be by the same mode as the offer or by a method equally as fast."},
{t:"Acceptance",s:"Class 2",q:"Conditional acceptance becomes binding",o:["immediately it is communicated","when the condition is fulfilled","only if reduced to writing","never, under any circumstances","when the offeror dies"],a:1,
 w:"‘I will buy it, but only if you deliver it’ is not acceptance. If the condition is performed within any time frame prescribed, the contract becomes automatically binding; if the time lapses first, the offer may be gone."},
{t:"Acceptance",s:"Class 2",q:"An offer may be terminated in all of the following ways except",o:["revocation by the offeror before acceptance","rejection by the offeree","lapse of the time prescribed","payment of consideration by the offeree","death of the offeror where the offeree has notice"],a:3,
 w:"Furnishing consideration moves a transaction towards a contract, not away from one. The recognised modes are revocation, rejection, lapse of time, failure of a condition, counter-offer and death."},
{t:"Acceptance",s:"Class 2",q:"A musician makes an offer to perform at your show and then dies before you accept. Compare a contract concerning land where a party dies. The distinction is that",o:["death always terminates an offer","death never terminates an offer","death terminates an offer for personal performance but successors may perform a property contract","only the offeree’s death matters","the offer converts into a deed on death"],a:2,
 w:"Where the offer is for personal service, the offeror’s death ends it. Where successors can step in and perform, as with land, the offer need not die with the party."},
/* ── Consideration ── */
{t:"Consideration",s:"Class 2 · Slide 1.2",q:"Consideration is best defined as",o:["the price paid in exchange for the other party’s promise","the written record of the contract","the signature of the parties","the profit made on the transaction","the court’s valuation of the bargain"],a:0,
 w:"Currie v. Misa describes it as some right, interest, profit or benefit to one party, or some forbearance, detriment, loss or responsibility borne by the other."},
{t:"Consideration",s:"Class 2",q:"A promise not supported by consideration is",o:["a gratuitous promise, and is not enforceable unless made under seal","automatically enforceable","void for illegality","voidable at the promisor’s option","enforceable only in the Magistrate Court"],a:0,
 w:"As the lecture put it, awoof is not a contract, unless it is under seal, since a deed needs no consideration."},
{t:"Consideration",s:"Class 2",q:"In Chappell & Co. v. Nestlé the chocolate wrappers were held to be",o:["worthless and therefore no consideration","sufficient consideration even though of little value","past consideration","illegal consideration","consideration that had not moved from the promisee"],a:1,
 w:"Consideration must be sufficient but need not be adequate. It is not for the court to price the parties’ bargain; the wrappers were part of what Nestlé asked for, so they counted."},
{t:"Consideration",s:"Class 2",q:"A and B make a contract for the benefit of C. C cannot sue to enforce it principally because",o:["C is not named in the document","consideration must move from the promisee and C furnished none","C is under 18","the contract was not in writing","C did not sign the contract"],a:1,
 w:"This is the doctrinal link between consideration and privity: only a person who has given something in exchange can hold the other party to his promise."},
{t:"Consideration",s:"Class 2",q:"A father promises his daughter his property, saying “I remember how you took care of me all these years”. The promise is unenforceable because",o:["it is illegal","the consideration is past","the consideration is inadequate","there is no writing","the daughter is a family member only"],a:1,
 w:"The care was already given before the promise was made, so it cannot be the price of that promise. The exception is a service rendered at the promisor’s request with the understanding that it would be paid for, as when you flag down a taxi."},
{t:"Consideration",s:"Slide 1.2 · Stilk v Myrick",q:"A promise to perform an existing obligation is not good consideration. This applies to a duty imposed by all except",o:["public duty","contract with the promisor","contract with a third party","a fresh undertaking to do more than one is bound to do","statute"],a:3,
 w:"Doing more than you are already bound to do IS consideration. Hiring a LASTMA officer to direct traffic he is already obliged to direct is not."},
{t:"Consideration",s:"Class 2",q:"A landowner accepts payment from a neighbour to endure noise from the neighbour’s boiler. The consideration furnished by the landowner is",o:["nothing at all, so there is no contract","the giving up of his right to peaceful enjoyment","past consideration","an illegal consideration","a gratuitous promise"],a:1,
 w:"Consideration need not be a thing or money. Surrendering a legal right (here the right to quiet enjoyment) is a real detriment and so good consideration."},
{t:"Consideration",s:"Class 4 · Pinnel's case",q:"Where a contracting party waives his rights under a contract wholly or in part, the waiver",o:["is always binding","is not binding unless consideration is given for it","must be registered","converts the contract into a deed","only binds companies"],a:1,
 w:"This is the rule in Pinnel’s case. It can be circumvented by promissory estoppel where the other party has changed his position in reliance on the waiver."},
{t:"Consideration",s:"Class 4",q:"The doctrine of promissory estoppel operates where",o:["a party has changed his position in reliance on a promise to waive contractual rights","a promise is made under seal","the parties are of unequal age","the contract is illegal","the consideration is inadequate"],a:0,
 w:"Equity does not look with favour on a man who promises relief to another and then goes back on the promise."},
/* ── Intention ── */
{t:"Intention",s:"Class 2",q:"In determining intention to create legal relations, the court applies",o:["a subjective test, asking the parties what they intended","an objective test, asking what a reasonable onlooker would assume","the officious bystander test only","the substantial benefit test","the business efficacy test"],a:1,
 w:"Parties deny everything once litigation starts, so the court looks at the circumstances and asks what a reasonable man or officious bystander would infer."},
{t:"Intention",s:"Class 2",q:"Which statement about the presumptions on intention is correct?",o:["Both presumptions are irrebuttable","Social/domestic agreements are presumed binding; commercial agreements are presumed not to be","Social/domestic agreements are presumed not binding; commercial agreements are presumed binding","Only written agreements carry a presumption","The presumption depends on the value of the contract"],a:2,
 w:"Both presumptions can be rebutted by evidence pointing the other way, a family arrangement drafted with obvious legal formality, for instance."},
/* ── Terms ── */
{t:"Terms",s:"Class 3",q:"Statements made during negotiation that are NOT legally binding are called",o:["conditions","warranties","mere representations","innominate terms","implied terms"],a:2,
 w:"A representation may still found an action in misrepresentation or in tort; it simply is not a term of the contract."},
{t:"Terms",s:"Class 3",q:"Applying the tests for distinguishing a term from a representation, a statement made at the early stage of negotiation is most likely",o:["a condition","a warranty","a representation","an implied term","an innominate term"],a:2,
 w:"Early statements are treated as representations; statements made later during formation are more likely to be terms."},
{t:"Terms",s:"Class 3",q:"A car dealer states the year model and mileage of a car to an ordinary consumer who then buys it. The statement is most likely",o:["a representation, since it was oral","a term, because the dealer has special knowledge and skill","void for mistake","an invitation to treat","past consideration"],a:1,
 w:"Reverse the parties (a private seller who knows nothing about cars making the same statement) and it is more likely to be a mere representation."},
{t:"Terms",s:"Class 3 · Bannerman v White",q:"A buyer tells a seller that he will not buy hops if they were treated with sulphur. The seller says they were not. This statement is",o:["a mere representation","a term, because its importance was communicated","an invitation to treat","void for illegality","an implied term by statute"],a:1,
 w:"Where a party makes clear that he would not have entered the contract but for the statement, the court treats the statement as a term."},
{t:"Terms",s:"Class 3",q:"Breach of a condition entitles the innocent party to",o:["damages only","terminate the contract and claim damages","specific performance only","an injunction only","nothing until the contract ends"],a:1,
 w:"A condition goes to the root of the contract: order a car and receive a bicycle. Breach of a warranty gives damages only."},
{t:"Terms",s:"Class 3",q:"You order a black car and a white one is delivered. This is most likely a breach of",o:["a condition","a warranty","an innominate term treated as a condition","the implied condition as to title","the implied condition as to sample"],a:1,
 w:"Colour does not go to the root of the contract and can be cured with money: you take the car and have it repainted. So damages, not termination."},
{t:"Terms",s:"Class 3",q:"To decide whether breach of an innominate term permits repudiation, the court applies",o:["the officious bystander test","the business efficacy test","the substantial benefit test","the caveat emptor rule","the parol evidence rule"],a:2,
 w:"If the breach deprives the innocent party of substantially the whole benefit of the contract, it is treated as a condition; if not, as a warranty."},
{t:"Terms",s:"Class 3",q:"Where the parties expressly label a term a condition, the court will",o:["always treat it as a condition without exception","usually agree, but may treat it as a warranty where money can cure the breach","always treat it as a warranty","refuse to enforce the contract","imply a term to the contrary"],a:1,
 w:"The court’s aim is to enforce the contract, not rewrite it, but it has held terms labelled conditions to be insufficiently serious where the breach could simply be paid for."},
{t:"Terms",s:"Class 3",q:"Courts are generally reluctant to imply terms into a contract because of",o:["the parol evidence rule","the principle of party autonomy","the doctrine of privity","the rule in Pinnel’s case","the ultra vires doctrine"],a:1,
 w:"Parties are expected to take care of their own agreements. The business of the court is to enforce the contract, not to write it for them."},
{t:"Terms",s:"Class 3",q:"You order rice and assorted meat for ₦10,000 and the food turns out to be rotten and full of stones. The court will most likely imply a term that the food be fresh using",o:["the business efficacy test","the officious bystander test","the substantial benefit test","the parol evidence rule","the postal rule"],a:1,
 w:"Asked whether the parties would have included such a term had they thought about it, the officious bystander answers ‘of course’."},
{t:"Terms",s:"Class 3",q:"A mechanic hired to fix a car on site finds it must be towed to his workshop. The court implies a term that towing be paid for using",o:["the officious bystander test","the business efficacy test","the substantial benefit test","custom and usage","the Sale of Goods Act"],a:1,
 w:"Business efficacy asks what is necessary to make the contract workable at all. Without towing the contract simply cannot be performed."},
{t:"Terms",s:"Class 3",q:"Agency fees on the lease of a house may be read into a contract even where the parties never mentioned them. This is a term implied by",o:["statute","the courts under the officious bystander test","custom or trade usage","the parties’ express agreement","the doctrine of privity"],a:2,
 w:"The practice must be recognised, notorious and well known in the industry, and it will not be implied where the parties have excluded it."},
/* ── Sale of Goods Act ── */
{t:"Sale of Goods",s:"Class 3 · S.12",q:"Section 12 of the Sale of Goods Act implies a condition as to title, together with warranties as to",o:["merchantable quality and fitness","quiet possession and freedom from undisclosed charges","correspondence with description and sample","price and delivery date","time and place of payment"],a:1,
 w:"You cannot sell what you do not have. Customs officers repeatedly stopping a buyer over unpaid duties is the lecture’s example of a breach of quiet possession."},
{t:"Sale of Goods",s:"Class 3 · S.13",q:"Buying a fridge online from a picture only, which arrives as a miniature ornament, is a breach of the implied condition as to",o:["title","description","satisfactory quality","fitness for purpose","sample"],a:1,
 w:"Where goods are sold by description they must correspond exactly with it. A sale may still be by description even where the buyer has seen the goods."},
{t:"Sale of Goods",s:"Class 3 · S.14",q:"To rely on the implied condition of fitness for purpose the buyer must show that",o:["the goods were expensive","he made known the particular purpose and relied on the seller’s skill or judgment","the contract was in writing","the seller was a registered company","he inspected the goods before purchase"],a:1,
 w:"If you tell the seller the purpose but then ignore his advice and choose something else, you cannot rely on the condition. Both limbs must be satisfied."},
{t:"Sale of Goods",s:"Class 3 · S.14",q:"The general rule governing quality in a sale of goods is expressed in the maxim",o:["caveat emptor","ex turpi causa non oritur actio","consensus ad idem","non est factum","quantum meruit"],a:0,
 w:"Let the buyer beware: the buyer must do his own due diligence. Section 14 carves out exceptions where the seller sells in the course of business."},
{t:"Sale of Goods",s:"Class 3 · S.15",q:"Where goods are sold by sample, the implied conditions include all except",o:["the bulk must correspond with the sample in quality","the buyer must have a reasonable opportunity to compare bulk with sample","the goods must be free from latent defects","the sample must be retained by the seller for six months"],a:3,
 w:"There are three conditions only. Latent defects are covered because a defect on the face of the sample would have been seen and negotiated over."},
/* ── Capacity ── */
{t:"Capacity",s:"Class 4",q:"The general rule on capacity to contract is that",o:["nobody may contract without a lawyer","everybody is fully capable of entering into a contract, subject to exceptions","only adults over 21 may contract","only registered companies may contract","capacity depends on the value of the contract"],a:1,
 w:"The exceptions exist because the law evolves to protect those who may be victims: infants, insane persons, drunken persons, illiterates and corporations."},
{t:"Capacity",s:"Class 4",q:"Under the common law applicable to contract, an infant in Nigeria is a person under the age of",o:["12 years","14 years","18 years","21 years","25 years"],a:3,
 w:"Contracts by infants are governed by common law as altered by the Infants Relief Act 1874, under which a child is anyone below 21. Section 277 of the Child’s Rights Act 2003 sets 18, and has been adopted by 34 states."},
{t:"Capacity",s:"Class 4",q:"Which states have not domesticated the Child’s Rights Act 2003?",o:["Lagos and Ogun","Kano and Zamfara","Edo and Delta","Osun and Oyo","Ekiti and Ondo"],a:1,
 w:"34 states have adopted the Act, which defines a child as a person under 18. Kano and Zamfara have not."},
{t:"Capacity",s:"Class 4",q:"Necessaries are defined as goods suitable to",o:["the condition in life of the infant and his actual requirements at the time of sale and delivery","the wealth of the infant’s family","the current market price","the infant’s stated preferences","any goods the infant can afford"],a:0,
 w:"The category covers food, drink, clothing and shelter, and extends to items appropriate to the infant’s financial and social station."},
{t:"Capacity",s:"Class 4",q:"Under section 1 of the Infant’s Relief Act 1874, which contract made by an infant is absolutely void?",o:["A contract for school fees","A contract of apprenticeship","A contract for money lent or to be lent","A lease of property","A contract for clothing"],a:2,
 w:"The three void categories are money lent or to be lent, non-necessary goods supplied, and accounts stated (an IOU). A child cannot borrow from a bank."},
{t:"Capacity",s:"Class 4",q:"Contracts under which an infant acquires an interest in property of a permanent nature with continuing obligations (leases, partnerships, shares) are",o:["void","valid and binding unless repudiated during infancy or within a reasonable time of majority","illegal","unenforceable in all cases","enforceable only by the adult party"],a:1,
 w:"The validity rests in the child’s hands. On reaching 18 he must ratify; if he does not within a reasonable time, he can be free of liability."},
{t:"Capacity",s:"Class 4",q:"A beneficial contract of service binds an infant provided that",o:["it is registered","the contract as a whole is substantially to the infant’s advantage","it lasts less than a year","a guardian signs it","the infant is over 16"],a:1,
 w:"Some terms may look exploitative while others are clearly beneficial; the court looks at the contract as a whole. Education and apprenticeship are the standard examples."},
{t:"Capacity",s:"Class 4",q:"Contracting with a person who is underage is",o:["a criminal offence punishable by imprisonment","a civil matter concerning damages and enforceability","void and also a crime","enforceable only in the Sharia Court","never possible"],a:1,
 w:"Nobody goes to jail for it. Criminal law only enters where there is separate criminal conduct such as fraud."},
{t:"Capacity",s:"Class 5",q:"An insane person seeking to escape a contract must prove all except",o:["that owing to his mental state he did not understand the nature of the contract","that his condition was known to the other party","that the contract was disadvantageous to him"],a:2,
 w:"Only the two limbs are required. Contracts with mentally disordered persons are prima facie valid, and become voidable at that party’s option once both limbs are proved."},
{t:"Capacity",s:"Class 5",q:"Which precaution was NOT recommended where an insane person approaches you to contract?",o:["Bring a psychiatrist to assess their mental state","Bring their next of kin","Bring third-party witnesses","Proceed quickly before their condition changes","Bring a person to whom they can transfer their roles"],a:3,
 w:"The recommended safeguards are all about creating a reliable record of understanding and consent."},
{t:"Capacity",s:"Class 4 · Class 5",q:"A person of unsound mind or a drunkard remains liable for",o:["all contracts without exception","necessaries, at a reasonable price","contracts for money lent","commercial contracts only","nothing at all"],a:1,
 w:"Even while suffering the disorder, such a person must pay a reasonable price for goods and services suitable to his position in life. Contracts made or ratified during lucid intervals or on sobering up are fully binding."},
{t:"Capacity",s:"Class 4 · Class 5",q:"The ultra vires doctrine provides that",o:["a company can never be sued","companies must operate within their stated objectives, and acts beyond them may be void","directors are personally liable for all company debts","a company has no legal personality","a company may only contract under seal"],a:1,
 w:"A company registered under the Companies and Allied Matters legislation has legal personality independent of its members, but that personality is bounded by its objects."},
/* ── Illiterates ── */
{t:"Illiterates",s:"Class 5",q:"Under the illiterate jurat, the writer must do all except",o:["record the name, thumbprint and address of the illiterate","read out and explain the entire contract","have the signing witnessed","take the principal benefit under the contract","write exactly what the illiterate is to do"],a:3,
 w:"If the writer is the one benefiting most, the contract is void. The writer’s role is neutral: to record, explain and confirm understanding."},
{t:"Illiterates",s:"Class 5",q:"The penalty for failing to comply with the illiterate jurat procedure is",o:["₦100 fine, or an option of 6 months imprisonment","₦10,000 fine only","2 years imprisonment","forfeiture of the property","a caution from the court"],a:0,
 w:"Modest as it is, the more serious consequence is that the document may be unenforceable against the illiterate."},
{t:"Illiterates",s:"Class 5",q:"Where a document creates rights between a third party and an illiterate, it is enforceable",o:["in no circumstances","only if the third party can prove the illiterate was aware of the contract, and the writer did not benefit","only if the illiterate signed personally","only if witnessed by a lawyer","only against the writer"],a:1,
 w:"The illiterate may equally enforce the document himself, provided he can call evidence of what happened when it was prepared."},
/* ── Privity ── */
{t:"Privity",s:"Class 5 · Class 6",q:"Yaba LG contracts with a construction company, which buys a water tank from a manufacturer under a separate contract guaranteeing 95% efficiency. The tank underperforms and Yaba LG loses money. Yaba LG should",o:["sue the manufacturer directly","sue the construction company, whose lawyer may then join the manufacturer as a co-defendant","sue both in separate actions","sue nobody, as there is no remedy","report the matter to the police"],a:1,
 w:"The manufacturer is a stranger to the contract with Yaba LG. Joining it as co-defendant puts all parties and evidence in one case and avoids duplicate litigation."},
{t:"Privity",s:"Class 6",q:"Alex, given explicit authority by TechCorp to acquire components, signs a supply contract in his own name. TechCorp refuses to pay. The supplier",o:["cannot sue anyone","can sue TechCorp, since Alex acted as an agent with authority","can sue only Alex personally","must sue both jointly","must first obtain a court order against Alex"],a:1,
 w:"Whatever the agent does within his authority is deemed done by the principal. The agent need not even use the principal’s name; the letter of authority speaks for the principal."},
{t:"Privity",s:"Class 5 · Class 6",q:"All of the following are exceptions to privity of contract except",o:["agency","trusteeship","assignment","third-party insurance","conditional acceptance"],a:4,
 w:"Conditional acceptance belongs to the law of offer and acceptance. The recognised exceptions are agency, trusteeship, assignment, covenants relating to land, third-party insurance and statutory exceptions."},
{t:"Privity",s:"Class 6",q:"A beneficiary under a trust may sue the trustee who fails to remit the benefits, even though the beneficiary was not present when the trust was created. This is because",o:["the trust is a deed","a trust is an equitable assignment of the benefit of a contract to a third party","the beneficiary furnished consideration","trusts are governed by criminal law","the doctrine of privity has been abolished"],a:1,
 w:"Equity looks to the substance: the beneficiary is the intended recipient of the benefit, so equity gives him standing."},
/* ── Mistake ── */
{t:"Mistake",s:"Class 6",q:"You buy a used generator engine at Ladipo Market and find at home it produces less power than you hoped. In law this is",o:["a common mistake","a unilateral mistake","not a mistake at all, since you got what you asked for","a mutual mistake","misrepresentation"],a:2,
 w:"Mistake in law is narrower than in ordinary usage: it relates to the identity or existence of the subject matter. Had you specified a 500-capacity engine and received a 300, that would be mistake."},
{t:"Mistake",s:"Class 6",q:"An engineer and a supplier contract for a batch of steel beams destroyed by fire the night before, unknown to both. This is",o:["common mistake, and the contract is void","mutual mistake, and the contract is voidable","unilateral mistake, and the contract is voidable","frustration, and the contract is discharged","misrepresentation"],a:0,
 w:"Frustration concerns events after formation. Here the subject matter had already ceased to exist when the contract was signed, so no contract ever arose and neither party is at fault."},
{t:"Mistake",s:"Class 6",q:"Being sent to buy ₦200 MTN credit and buying ₦200 Airtel credit instead is the lecture’s illustration of",o:["common mistake","mutual mistake","unilateral mistake","innocent misrepresentation","frustration"],a:1,
 w:"The parties are at cross-purposes about the identity of the subject matter. The card itself works; it is the identity that is wrong."},
{t:"Mistake",s:"Class 6",q:"An engineer’s spreadsheet formula drops a zero and he bids ₦50,000 instead of ₦500,000. The client notices, realises it is an error, and immediately clicks Accept. The engineer",o:["must perform at ₦50,000, since he made the offer","can have the contract set aside for unilateral mistake","must perform but may claim damages","is guilty of misrepresentation","may claim indemnity only"],a:1,
 w:"Where one party is mistaken and the other knows and exploits the mistake, the contract can be set aside."},
{t:"Mistake",s:"Class 6",q:"At common law the general effect of an operative mistake is that the contract is",o:["voidable","void, as though it never existed","unenforceable but valid","illegal","discharged by frustration"],a:1,
 w:"Equity may still find a remedy where the mistake does not go to the very foundation of the contract."},
/* ── Misrepresentation ── */
{t:"Misrepresentation",s:"Class 7",q:"A misrepresentation must be a statement of fact and not of",o:["law, opinion or intention","price","quality","quantity","delivery date"],a:0,
 w:"Citizens are expected to know the law; ‘I think you can build three storeys’ is opinion; ‘I intend to renovate’ is intention. If someone wants to give you something, get a deed of gift."},
{t:"Misrepresentation",s:"Class 7",q:"The general rule is that silence does not amount to misrepresentation. The exception applies to contracts of",o:["sale of goods","utmost good faith (uberrimae fidei)","employment","carriage","construction"],a:1,
 w:"Insurance, land acquisition, shares, position of credit and family arrangements are the listed instances where non-disclosure can amount to misrepresentation."},
{t:"Misrepresentation",s:"Class 7",q:"A supplier honestly believes a machine is a 2024 model because of a manufacturer’s labelling error. The buyer’s remedy is",o:["rescission and damages","rescission and indemnity, but not damages","damages only","specific performance","nothing at all"],a:1,
 w:"Innocent misrepresentation involves no intention to deceive, so common law gives no damages. Equity supplies indemnity for foreseeable expenses instead."},
{t:"Misrepresentation",s:"Class 7",q:"A civil engineer tells a client a bridge can support 50 tons, having used outdated formulas and skipped the mandatory safety recalculation. This is",o:["fraudulent misrepresentation","innocent misrepresentation","negligent misrepresentation","common mistake","duress"],a:2,
 w:"Careless, with no reasonable grounds to believe the statement true. Remedy is rescission plus damages, and the professional may also lose his licence."},
{t:"Misrepresentation",s:"Class 7",q:"An agent describes property No. 19 using the specifications of property No. 21, which had already been assigned to another person by court order. This is",o:["innocent misrepresentation","negligent misrepresentation","fraudulent misrepresentation","common mistake","frustration"],a:2,
 w:"The statement was made knowingly, with intent to deceive. Remedy is rescission plus damages."},
{t:"Misrepresentation",s:"Class 7",q:"You spend ₦500,000 on logistics to collect an item in Sokoto which turns out not to meet specifications, the seller having been honestly mistaken. You then ship it to America and hire staff there. Indemnity will cover",o:["all of the losses","the ₦500,000 logistics cost only, being foreseeable","the American staffing costs only","nothing, since the misrepresentation was innocent","the value of the item only"],a:1,
 w:"Indemnity covers only expenses foreseeable at the time of the contract. The onward shipping and hiring were not foreseeable."},
{t:"Misrepresentation",s:"Class 7",q:"Damages for misrepresentation",o:["can never exceed the contract value","can exceed the contract value depending on the harm caused","are fixed by statute at 10% of the contract sum","are available for all three types of misrepresentation","are the same as indemnity"],a:1,
 w:"They may include medical costs, emotional trauma and exemplary damages. A product worth ₦2 million could produce damages of ₦50 million."},
/* ── Duress, undue influence, illegality ── */
{t:"Duress",s:"Class 9",q:"The essential elements of duress include all except",o:["a threat to life, body or goods before acceptance","lack of free will","illegitimate pressure","pressure that induced the contract","a written record of the threat"],a:4,
 w:"Evidence certainly helps (the class stressed documents, voice notes and photographs) but a written record is not an element of the doctrine."},
{t:"Duress",s:"Class 9",q:"A mechanical engineer in a partnership demands 60% at the critical point of a project, knowing the others cannot proceed without him. This is an example of",o:["physical duress","economic duress","duress to goods","undue influence","illegality"],a:1,
 w:"Economic duress is the hardest type to prove precisely because it must be distinguished from hard but legitimate negotiation."},
{t:"Duress",s:"Class 8",q:"The principle that a person who signs a document is bound by its terms is expressed as",o:["non est factum","ex turpi causa","quantum meruit","consensus ad idem","restitutio in integrum"],a:0,
 w:"The signature binds, unless it was obtained under duress, in which case the contract is voidable because free consent was absent."},
{t:"Duress",s:"Class 9",q:"Okonkwo v. Cooperative Commerce Bank PLC is authority that",o:["consent obtained under improper pressure may render a transaction voidable where coercion is established","all bank contracts are void","duress must be physical to be actionable","economic duress is not recognised in Nigeria","a company cannot exert duress"],a:0,
 w:"Note the word voidable, not void: the aggrieved party chooses whether to set the transaction aside. Coercion must still be established, and the class stressed how hard that evidential burden is."},
{t:"Duress",s:"Class 9",q:"Undue influence is presumed in all of the following relationships except",o:["solicitor and client","doctor and patient","trustee and beneficiary","spiritual adviser and follower","landlord and tenant"],a:4,
 w:"A landlord may certainly exercise undue influence in fact (the class gave the example of constant rent increases designed to frustrate a tenant) but the relationship does not raise the presumption."},
{t:"Duress",s:"Class 9",q:"A fiduciary relationship between the parties",o:["automatically proves undue influence","raises a presumption which must still be tested by the court","is irrelevant to undue influence","makes the contract void","converts the contract into a deed"],a:1,
 w:"The court listens to the parties and determines whether advantage was actually taken. The presumption shifts the burden; it does not decide the case."},
{t:"Illegality",s:"Class 8",q:"Victor pays Barnabas ₦5 million of an agreed ₦10 million to rig an election. Barnabas does not deliver. Victor",o:["can sue for the balance","can sue for specific performance","can recover the ₦5 million already paid","cannot go to court at all, both parties lose","can report the matter and recover through the police"],a:3,
 w:"Ex turpi causa non oritur actio. The parties are in pari delicto, so benefits received are irrecoverable: the ₦5 million paid is gone and the balance is unenforceable."},
{t:"Illegality",s:"Class 8",q:"The maxim ex turpi causa non oritur actio means",o:["let the buyer beware","no action arises from a dishonourable cause","as much as earned","a meeting of the minds","it is not my deed"],a:1,
 w:"It is the reason a court will neither enforce an illegal contract nor grant relief under it: judicial integrity requires that courts not become instruments of illegality."},
{t:"Illegality",s:"Class 8",q:"Which is NOT given as a characteristic of a void contract?",o:["Void from inception","Necessarily involves criminal conduct","You cannot enforce rights under it","Impossibility of performance can make it void"],a:1,
 w:"A contract with a minor for non-necessaries is void without anyone committing a crime. Illegal contracts go further: the law actively prohibits the act and penalties may follow."},
{t:"Illegality",s:"Class 8",q:"The distinction between a voidable and a void contract is that a voidable contract",o:["is void from inception","exists, and the aggrieved party may either continue with it or set it aside","can never be rescinded","binds only one party","must be in writing"],a:1,
 w:"The choice belongs to the aggrieved party. A void contract, by contrast, never had legal force at all."},
/* ── Discharge & remedies ── */
{t:"Discharge",s:"Class 9",q:"A contractor given two years to build a hall is still on the foundation after two years. This is discharge by",o:["performance","agreement","breach","frustration","operation of law"],a:2,
 w:"A fundamental breach entitles the innocent party to terminate the contract and sue for damages."},
{t:"Discharge",s:"Class 9",q:"Which of the following would NOT amount to discharge by frustration?",o:["The hall booked for a reception burns down","A musician hired for a wedding dies","A change in the law renders the contract illegal","The contractor finds the job less profitable than expected","A natural disaster makes performance impossible"],a:3,
 w:"Frustration requires that performance become impossible or radically different, not merely more expensive or less attractive."},
{t:"Discharge",s:"Class 9",q:"Bankruptcy, merger and limitation periods are examples of discharge by",o:["performance","agreement","breach","operation of law","frustration"],a:3,
 w:"These end contractual obligations by force of law rather than by anything the parties do."},
{t:"Remedies",s:"Class 9",q:"Damages that are pre-agreed and stated in the contract itself are called",o:["general damages","special damages","nominal damages","exemplary damages","liquidated damages"],a:4,
 w:"General damages flow naturally from the breach; special damages must be specifically proved; nominal damages mark a breach with no real loss; exemplary damages make an example of the wrongdoer."},
{t:"Remedies",s:"Class 9",q:"Specific performance will generally be granted where",o:["the claimant asks for it","damages would be inadequate, as with unique land or rare property","the contract is under seal","the defendant is solvent","the breach is trivial"],a:1,
 w:"It is an equitable order compelling actual performance, reserved for cases where no amount of money can compensate."},
{t:"Remedies",s:"Class 9",q:"A contractor has completed 60% of a structure before the contract is discharged. The court may order payment for the work done under",o:["restitution","quantum meruit","rescission","injunction","liquidated damages"],a:1,
 w:"Quantum meruit means ‘as much as earned’: payment for work actually performed where the contract cannot be fully completed."},
{t:"Remedies",s:"Class 9",q:"Your neighbour’s construction threatens your building’s foundation. The appropriate remedy to seek quickly is",o:["damages","specific performance","an injunction","rescission","quantum meruit"],a:2,
 w:"An injunction restrains a party from committing or continuing a breach; it is the remedy for harm that is ongoing or imminent rather than already done."},
{t:"Remedies",s:"Class 9",q:"The remedy that requires restoration of benefits unjustly obtained is",o:["restitution","quantum meruit","injunction","specific performance","liquidated damages"],a:0,
 w:"Restitution orders the party to give back what he gained, putting the other party in the position he was in before the contract."},
{t:"Remedies",s:"Class 9",q:"Which is listed as a challenge to obtaining contractual remedies in Nigeria?",o:["Excess of judges","Delay in obtaining judgments and difficulty enforcing court orders","Absence of any law on remedies","Prohibition of equitable remedies","Refusal of the Supreme Court to hear contract cases"],a:1,
 w:"The class also listed high litigation costs, difficulty proving the quantum of damages, and the limited availability of equitable remedies to a claimant who has not acted equitably."},
{t:"Remedies",s:"Class 9",q:"Bilante International Limited v. NDIC affirms that",o:["parties are bound by the terms of their agreement, and remedies depend on the nature of the breach and the contract terms","all contracts must be registered","damages are always the only remedy","only the Federal High Court may hear contract disputes","oral contracts are unenforceable"],a:0,
 w:"The case restates the sanctity of contract: the court enforces the bargain the parties made, and what remedy follows depends on how serious the breach was and what the contract itself provides."}
]};
